From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] Modeling Algorithms - BRepOffset_MakeOffset visits the arc-join
 offset faces in the order they were bound

BRepOffset_MakeOffset::BuildOffsetByArc builds a BRepOffset_Offset for every face, every convex
edge (a tube) and every vertex (a sphere) of the shape and keeps them in

  NCollection_DataMap<TopoDS_Shape, BRepOffset_Offset, TopTools_ShapeMapHasher> MapSF;

It then iterates MapSF to register each offset face as a root of myInitOffsetFace and
myImageOffset. MakeShells passes myImageOffset.Roots() to BRepTools_Quilt in that order, and the
quilt keeps the faces in the order it is given them, so the order of the roots is the order of the
faces of the result.

std::hash<TopoDS_Shape> is the address of the TShape (TopoDS_Shape.hxx), so the iteration order is
decided by where the allocator put the shapes. It differs between processes and, as the heap
evolves, between two builds in one process. Every arc-join offset (BRepOffsetAPI_MakeOffsetShape
and BRepOffsetAPI_MakeThickSolid with GeomAbs_Arc) therefore returns its faces in a different
order from run to run, and the volume, which BRepGProp sums over the faces in the order the shape
holds them, moves in its last digits.

Nothing is parallel (the process has one thread and BOPAlgo_Options::GetParallelMode() is false)
and nothing is read uninitialised: with the roots visited in a fixed order the same inputs give the
same bits, in every process and every build.

Measured on macOS arm64 against the V8_0_1 asset with its carried patches, 60 fresh processes per
row (Scripts/repro/3003-offset-roots-hash-order, run.sh):

  request                                       volumes   BinTools dumps
  box 10, offset +1, GeomAbs_Arc                      6               60
  box 20, thick solid 2.0, top face open              3               60
  box 20, thick solid 2.0, nothing open               5               60
  box 10, offset -1, GeomAbs_Arc                      1               57
  cylinder, offset +1, GeomAbs_Arc                    1               28
  box 10, offset +1, GeomAbs_Intersection             1                1

"Volumes" and "BinTools dumps" count the distinct volumes, and the distinct hashes of the bit-exact
BinTools dump of the result. The rows at one volume and 57 or 28 dumps return the same solid with
its faces in another order, and sum to the same volume because their faces are symmetric enough to
add exactly in any order. The last row does not go through BuildOffsetByArc and is already
reproducible. Recompiling the unmodified file with the kernel's own flags and linking it ahead of
the archive reproduces the drift, which rules out the build; with this change every row is 1 and 1.
In one process, 32 builds of the first row with a different amount of heap held before each give 32
different face orders without the change and 1 with it.

Over a battery of 72 requests (nine shapes, eight requests each) run in 20 processes, 29 returned
more than one distinct result before the change and none after. In 26 of them only the order of the
faces differed; 3 are the case below. The other 69 return the same outcome with and without the
change.

The change records the order the entries are bound in (the faces as MakeOffsetFaces binds them,
that is BRepLib::SortFaces over myFaceComp and then the faces BRepOffset_Analyse added, then the
tubes, then the spheres) and walks that list, looking each entry up in MapSF, from which ToContext
may have removed it. Which offset faces are built, and how, does not change.

For an input whose success depends on the order the roots arrive in, the result is now the same
every time instead of depending on the heap. The one found is the fuse of two boxes, which keeps
its coplanar faces split: BRepOffsetAPI_MakeOffsetShape with GeomAbs_Arc and +1 returns a solid in
8 of 20 processes and, in the others, reports IsDone() with a null shape; over 300 shuffled root
orders 125 succeed. The order chosen here is one of the failing ones for that input, so it now
fails every time. That order dependence belongs to the intersection stage that follows and is not
addressed here.

A GTest builds the same offset 32 times with a different amount of heap held before each and
compares the order of the faces and the volume with the first build. It fails without the change
(15 of 15 runs) and passes with it (15 of 15); the other 19 tests in the file pass either way.

Reported as SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift#3003.

